<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finance-loans" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Annuities and Amortized Loans</title>

  <!-- This section is a remix of PCC, Sekhon and Bloom material, and my own original writing. -->

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find the present value of an annuity and the payment from a payout annuity.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find the payment, total interest, and remaining balance on an installment loan.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculate a credit card balance and minimum payment.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous section, we studied savings plans. In this section, we turn to <term>payout annuities</term><idx><h>annuities</h><h>payout</h></idx> and <term>conventional loans</term>. These two financial concepts use the same formula: the <term>present value of an annuity</term> formula.  
    </p>
    <p>
      In a payout annuity, the account is opened with a lump sum, and money is withdrawn from the account at regular intervals.  The money remaining in the account earns interest.  After a fixed amount of time, the account will be empty. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Conventional loans are also called <term>amortized loans</term> or <term>installment loans</term>.<idx><h>loans</h><h>amortized</h></idx><idx><h>amortized loans</h><see>loans, amortized</see></idx><idx><h>installment loans</h><see>loans, amortized</see></idx><idx><h>conventional loans</h><see>loans, amortized</see></idx> They are designed so that the payment amount remains the same over the life (term) of the loan. Examples include student loans, car loans, and home mortgages. The techniques we use to analyze these loans do not apply to payday loans, add-on loans, or other loan types where the interest is calculated up front. 
    </p>
 
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-present-value-annuities">
    <title>Present Value of an Annuity</title>
    <p>
      The <term>present value</term> of an annuity is the amount of money we would need now in order to be able to make the payments in the annuity in the future. In other words, the present value is the value now of a future stream of payments.
      To better understand what this means, consider the following example.
    </p>

      <p>
        Suppose Carlos owns a small business and employs an assistant manager to help him run the business.  Assume it is January 1 now.  Carlos plans to pay his assistant manager a $1,000 bonus at the end of this year and another $1,000 bonus at the end of the following year. Carlos's business earned good profits last year, so he wants to put the money for his assistant's future bonuses into a savings account now.  The money he puts in now will earn interest at the rate of 4% per year compounded annually while in the savings account. 
      </p>
      <p>
        How much money should Carlos put into the savings account now so that he will be able to withdraw $1,000 one year from now and another $1,000 two years from now? At first, this sounds like a sinking fund, but the situation is actually different.  In a sinking fund, we put money into the fund with periodic payments to accumulate a specified lump sum that is the future value at the end of a specified time period.
        In this case we want to put a lump sum into the savings account now, so that lump sum is our principal, <m>P</m>.  Then we want to withdraw that amount as a series of periodic payments; here the withdrawals are an annuity with $1,000 payments at the end of each of two years.
        This scenario is illustrated in <xref ref="fig-amortization-cartoon"/>.
      </p>   
      <figure xml:id="fig-amortization-cartoon">
        <caption>How much should Carlos invest now to be able to withdraw two payments of $1,000 at the end of the next two years and end with a zero account balance?</caption>
        <image xml:id="img-amortization-cartoon" width="85%">
          <description><p>A timeline from 0 to 2 years: a deposit of $P at time 0, and withdrawals of $1,000 at the end of years 1 and 2.</p></description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont, x=3cm, y=1cm]
              % timeline with year ticks
              \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2,0);
              \foreach \x in {0,1,2}
                \draw[thick] (\x,0) -- (\x,0.25) node[above] {\x};
              % row labels, right-aligned left of the timeline
              \node[anchor=east] at (-0.15,0.55) {Time (years)};
              \node[anchor=east] at (-0.15,-0.8) {Deposit of Present Value:};
              \node[anchor=east] at (-0.15,-1.7) {Withdrawals for Payments:};
              % amounts under the matching year
              \node at (0,-0.8) {\$P};
              \node at (1,-1.7) {\$1,000};
              \node at (2,-1.7) {\$1,000};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        To answer this question we use the compound interest formula from <xref ref="sec-finance-compound-interest"/> with <m>r = 0.04</m> and <m>n = 1</m> for annual compounding to determine the present value of each payment of $1,000.
        Consider the first payment of $1,000 at the end of year 1. Let <m>P_1</m> be its present value:
        <md>
           \$1{,}000 = P_1(1.04)^1 \Rightarrow P_1 \approx \$961.54.
        </md>
        Now consider the second payment of $1,000 at the end of year 2. Let <m>P_2</m> be its present value:
        <md>
          \$1{,}000 = P_2(1.04)^2 \Rightarrow P_2 \approx \$924.56.
        </md>
        To make the $1,000 payments at the specified times in the future, the amount that Carlos needs to deposit now is the present value 
        <md>
          P = P_1 + P_2 = \$961.54 + \$924.56 = \$1{,}886.10.
        </md>
        <alert>Note:</alert> Carlos needs to save less than $2,000 now because his deposit will earn interest while it is waiting to be withdrawn. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculation above was useful to illustrate the meaning of the present value of an annuity, but it is not an efficient way to calculate the present value.  If we were to have a large number of annuity payments, the step-by-step calculation would be long and tedious.  
        Instead, we use the formula shown below. This formula is derived in a similar way to the way we derived the formula for the future value of an annuity in <xref ref="subsec-derivation-future-value-of-annuity"/>. The details are omitted here.
      </p>
      <assemblage>
        <title>Present Value of an Ordinary Annuity</title>
        <idx>present value of an annuity</idx>
        <idx><h>financial formulas</h><h>present value of an annuity</h></idx>
        <idx><h>annuities</h><h>present value</h></idx>
        <p>
          If payments of <m>d</m> dollars are made <m>n</m> times a year for <m>t</m> years, at an annual interest rate <m>r</m> compounded <m>n</m> times per year, then the present value <m>P</m> of the annuity is
          <md>
            P = d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{-nt}}{\frac{r}{n}}\right].
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Glossary:
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p><m>P</m> is the value of the annuity at the beginning (or the principal, or amount of the loan)</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>d</m> is the annuity payment (or disbursement); for a loan, this is the monthly payment, annual payment, etc.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>r</m> is the annual interest rate in decimal form</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>n</m> is the number of payments (and compounding periods) per year</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>t</m> is the number of years</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </assemblage>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            After retiring, you want to be able to take $1,000 every month for a total of 20 years from your retirement account.  The account earns 6% interest.  How much will you need in your account when you retire?
          </p>
        </statement>          
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is an annuity question where we seek the present value (or lump sum) needed to generate the sequence of anticipated payments. 
            In this example, <m>d = \$1{,}000</m>, <m>r = 0.06</m>, <m>n = 12</m>, since we want monthly withdrawals, and <m>t = 20</m> because we are taking withdrawals for 20 years. <m>P</m> is unknown.
            <md>
              <mrow> P =\amp d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{-nt}}{\frac{r}{n}}\right] </mrow>
              <mrow>  =\amp \$1{,}000 \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.06}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 20}}{\frac{0.06}{12}}\right]</mrow>
              <mrow>  =\amp \$1{,}000 \left[\frac{1-1.005^{-240}}{0.005}\right] </mrow>
              <mrow>  =\amp \$1{,}000(139.580772) </mrow>
              <mrow>  \approx\amp \$139{,}580.77. </mrow>
          </md>
            That is, you will need to have $139,580.77 in your account when you retire.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <p>
        <alert>Note:</alert> In annuity problems the compounding frequency is not always explicitly given, but it is implicitly defined by how often you take the withdrawals.
      </p>
      <p>
        The present value of an annuity formula can also be used to find the annuity payment, as shown in the next example.
      </p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            You know you will have $500,000 in your retirement account when you retire.  You want to be able to take monthly withdrawals from the account for a total of 30 years.  Your retirement account earns 8% interest.  How much will you be able to withdraw each month?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is an annuity question where we know the present value <m>P</m> but seek to find the payment (or disbursement) <m>d</m>.
            In this example, <m>P = \$500{,}000</m>, <m>r = 0.08</m>, <m>n = 12</m>, since we want monthly withdrawals, and <m>t = 30</m> because we are taking withdrawals for 30 years. The withdrawal amount <m>d</m> is unknown.
          <md>
            <mrow>  \$500{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.08}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 30}}{\frac{0.08}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>  \$500{,}000 =\amp d(136.283494) </mrow>
            <mrow>  d =\amp\frac{\$500{,}000}{136.283494} </mrow>
            <mrow>  \approx\amp\$3{,}668.82 </mrow>
          </md>
          You will be able to withdraw $3,668.82 per month for 30 years.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-amortization">
    <title>Installment Loans</title>
    <p>
      Because the payment on an installment loan stays the same over the life of the loan, the amounts going toward the principal (amount owed) and toward the interest must vary. At the beginning of the loan, the amount owed is the largest, so the interest charged is also the largest. As the loan is repaid, the amount owed shrinks, and so does the interest. Therefore, at the beginning of an installment loan, payments go mostly toward interest; toward the end, they go mostly toward principal.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-installment-loan-interest-principal">
      <caption>Interest and principal paid each year on a 10-year, $34,000 loan at 10% APR with monthly payments of $449.31 ($5,391.72 per year). In the first year, $3,305.92 went to interest and $2,085.80 to principal; in the last year, only $281.04 went to interest and $5,110.68 to principal. Over 10 years, a total of $53,917.20 was paid on the $34,000 loan.</caption>
      <image xml:id="img-installment-loan-interest-principal" width="85%">
        <description><p>A stacked bar graph with one bar for each year of the loan, each bar the same height ($5,391.72 paid that year). The interest part of each bar shrinks from about $3,306 in year 1 to about $281 in year 10, while the principal part grows from about $2,086 to about $5,111: most of the payment goes to interest at the beginning of the loan and most goes to principal at the end.</p></description>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
          \begin{axis}[
              ybar stacked,
              bar width=0.6cm,
              width=12cm, height=8cm,
              axis lines=left,
              axis line style={thick, -}, tick style={thick},
              xmin=0.3, xmax=10.7,
              ymin=0, ymax=6000,
              xtick={1,...,10},
              ytick={0,1000,...,6000},
              yticklabel={\$\pgfmathprintnumber[1000 sep={,}]{\tick}},
              scaled y ticks=false,
              ymajorgrids=true,
              xlabel={Year of loan},
              ylabel={Amount paid that year},
              ticklabel style={font=\figfont},
              legend style={at={(0.5,1.03)}, anchor=south, legend columns=-1,
                draw=none, font=\figfont, /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=0.5cm}},
            ]
            % yearly interest and principal: $34,000 at 10% APR, 120 payments of $449.31
            \addplot[fill=CustomB1, draw=black] coordinates {
              (1,3306.13) (2,3087.74) (3,2846.49) (4,2579.95) (5,2285.52)
              (6,1960.28) (7,1600.97) (8,1204.01) (9,765.50) (10,281.08)};
            \addplot[fill=CustomB1!15, draw=black] coordinates {
              (1,2085.59) (2,2303.98) (3,2545.23) (4,2811.77) (5,3106.20)
              (6,3431.44) (7,3790.75) (8,4187.71) (9,4626.22) (10,5110.64)};
            \legend{Interest, Principal}
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
    </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Amortized loan questions are solved using the same present value of an annuity formula used for payout annuities. The primary difference is one of context. The equations can be solved for <m>P</m> or <m>d</m> as before, but the interpretation of the result is different.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
      <p>
        You can afford $200 per month as a car payment. If you can get an auto loan at 3% APR for 60 months (5 years), how expensive a car can you afford? In other words, what loan amount can you pay off with $200 per month?
        How much interest would you pay for this loan?
      </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
      <p>
        In this example, we are looking for <m>P</m>, the starting amount of the loan. We are given values for <m>d=\$200</m>, the monthly loan payment, <m>r=0.03</m>, the annual interest rate, and <m>t=5</m>, since we're making monthly payments for 5 years. 
        Since we're doing monthly payments, <m>n=12</m>. Then:
        <md>
          <mrow>P \amp= d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{-nt}}{\frac{r}{n}} \right]</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp= \$200\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.03}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 5}}{\frac{0.03}{12}}\right] </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp= \$200\left[\frac{1-(1.0025)^{-60}}{0.0025}\right]</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp\approx\$11{,}130.47</mrow>
        </md>
        That is, you can afford a maximum loan of $11,130.47. If you have a down payment, you can add that to get the value of the car you can buy. Any closing costs for the loan should also be taken into consideration.
      </p>    
      <p>
        To find the amount of interest you will pay for this loan, calculate the total of all your payments.
        <md>\$200(5)(12)=\$12{,}000</md>
        Then take the difference between the total payments and the loan amount.
        <md>\$12{,}000-\$11{,}130.47=\$869.53</md>
        In this case, you would be paying $869.53 in interest.
      </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Teresa wants to buy a car that costs $15,000. She has $3,000 saved for the car and plans to finance the rest. She found a 3-year loan at 2.75% APR and a 5-year loan at 4% APR. How much would her monthly car payment be for the first loan option? For the second? How does the interest Teresa would pay compare for each loan?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First note that Teresa is making a down payment, so the amount of the loan she needs is <m>\$15{,}000-\$3{,}000=\$12{,}000</m>. This is the value of <m>P</m>. We solve for the unknown payment, <m>d</m> using the present value of an annuity formula. 
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Loan Option 1:</em> For the first option, <m>P = \$12{,}000</m>, <m>n = 12</m>, <m>r = 0.0275</m>, and <m>t = 3</m>. Solving for <m>d</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>  \$12{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.0275}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 3}}{\frac{0.0275}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>  \$12{,}000 =\amp d(34.51708) </mrow>
            <mrow>  d =\amp\frac{\$12{,}000}{34.51708} </mrow>
            <mrow>  \approx\amp\$347.65 </mrow>
          </md>
          For the first loan option, Teresa's payment is $347.65 per month. Over the course of this loan, she would pay
          <md>
            12(3)(\$347.65) - \$12{,}000 = \$515.40
          </md>
          of interest.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Loan Option 2:</em> For the second option, the values of <m>P</m> and <m>n</m> are the same, but <m>r = 0.04</m>, and <m>t = 5</m>. Solving again for <m>d</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>  \$12{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.04}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 5}}{\frac{0.04}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>  \$12{,}000 =\amp d(54.29906890) </mrow>
            <mrow>  d =\amp\frac{\$12{,}000}{54.29906890} </mrow>
            <mrow>  \approx\amp\$221.00 </mrow>
          </md>
          For the second loan option, Teresa's payment is $221.00 per month. Over the course of this loan, she would pay
          <md>
            12(5)(\$221.00) - \$12{,}000 = \$1{,}260.00
          </md>
          of interest. That is, for the second loan her monthly payment would be less, but because she is paying off the loan more slowly and at a higher rate, she pays more interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
      So far, we have looked at car loans. Student loans and home mortgages are calculated in the same way. Here is an example of a mortgage payment.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
      <p>
        You want to take out a $140,000 mortgage (home loan). The interest rate on the loan is 6%, and the loan is for 30 years. How much will your monthly payments be? What percentage of your total payments will go toward interest?
      </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
      <p>
        In this example we have: <m>r=0.06</m>, <m>n=12</m>, <m>t=30</m>, and <m>P=\$140{,}000</m>. We will use the present value of an annuity formula to solve for <m>d</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>  \$140{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.06}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 30}}{\frac{0.06}{12}}\right]</mrow>
          <mrow>  \$140{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{1-1.005^{-360}}{0.005}\right]</mrow>
          <mrow>  \$140{,}000 =\amp d(166.791614) </mrow>
          <mrow>  d =\amp\frac{\$140{,}000}{166.791614} </mrow>
          <mrow>  \approx\amp\$839.37 </mrow>
        </md>
        You would make payments of $839.37 per month for 30 years. 
      </p>
      <p>
        To find out what percentage of the total will go toward interest, we need to total up all of the payments.
        <md>\$839.37(30)(12)=\$302{,}173.20</md>
        Then, subtract the loan amount from the total payments.
        <md>\$302{,}173.20-\$140{,}000=\$162{,}173.20</md>
        In this case, you would be paying $162,173.20 in interest over the life of the loan. 
      </p>   
      <p>     
        To find the percentage, we divide the interest by the total amount paid.
        <md>\frac{\$162{,}173.20}{\$302{,}173.20} \approx 0.5367</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        About 53.7% of the total is being paid toward interest.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
      With loans, it is sometimes desirable to determine what the remaining loan balance<idx><h>loans</h><h>remaining balance</h></idx> will be after some number of years. For example, if you purchase a home and plan to sell it in five years, you might want to know how much of the loan balance you will have paid off and how much you will have to pay from the sale.
    </p>
    <p>
      To determine the remaining loan balance after some number of years, we first need to calculate the payment amount, if we don't already know it. Remember that only a portion of your loan payments go toward the loan balance; a portion is going to go toward interest. For example, if your payments were $1,000 a month, after a year you will <em>not</em> have paid off $12,000 of the loan balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      To determine the remaining loan balance, we can think <q>how much of the loan balance can these payments pay off in the remaining time?</q>
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
      <p>
        If a 30-year mortgage at an interest rate of 6% APR has payments of $1,000 a month, what will the loan balance be in 5 years?
      </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To determine this, we need to think backwards. We are looking for the amount of the loan that can be paid off by $1,000 per month in the remaining 25 years. In other words, we're looking for <m>P</m> when:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p><m>d=\$1{,}000</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>r=0.06</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>n=12</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>t=25</m>, since we'd be making monthly payments for 25 more years.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Solving for <m>P</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>P\amp=\$1{,}000\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.06}{12}\right)^{-12\cdot 25}}{\frac{0.06}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\$1{,}000\left[\frac{1-(1.005)^{-300}}{0.005}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp\approx\$155{,}206.86</mrow>
          </md>
          The loan balance with 25 years remaining on the loan will be $155,206.86.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
      Sometimes answering remaining balance questions requires two steps, both of which we have done in this section:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate the monthly payment on the loan
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate the remaining loan balance based on the <em>remaining time</em> on the loan
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Credit Cards</title>
      <p>
        In this section we will also briefly cover credit cards.<idx>credit cards</idx>
        Credit cards are useful to many people. They can be used to build a credit score, as a short-term loan, and as an alternative to physical cash. It is highly advisable to fully pay off a credit card each month because the interest rates are much higher than the conventional installment loans we discussed earlier. There can also be many expensive fees applied when carrying a balance. Because of these higher rates, it can be very easy to get into a lot of debt quickly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Credit cards are a common source of loan money, and therefore a common source of debt which must be repaid.  There are fewer and lower barriers to obtaining and using a credit card than there are to obtaining, for example, a mortgage.  As such, there are some drawbacks both in terms of typically higher interest rates, as well as some structural differences.
      </p>
      <example>
        <statement>
        <p>
          Amira gets a credit card with 17.99% APR compounding daily. She uses the card to buy a $900 plane ticket.  She does not make any payments during the interest-free grace period. Her first payment is due 35 days after the grace period ended. Following the fine print in the credit card agreement, the minimum payment<idx><h>credit cards</h><h>minimum payment</h></idx> is calculated to be 1% of the outstanding balance (after interest) or $25, whichever is larger. What is her outstanding balance? What is her minimum payment? If she pays the minimum payment what will her new balance be?
        </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            First we will see what she owes with the 17.99% APR using the compound interest formula for 35 days:
            <md>\$900\left(1+\frac{0.1799}{365}\right)^{35} \approx \$915.66</md>
            With interest, when her first payment is due, Amira owes $915.66.
          </p>
          <p>
             Next, we find one percent of the new balance, which is
             <md>\$915.66(0.01) \approx \$9.16</md>
            Since $25 is larger than $9.16, her minimum payment is $25. If she makes the minimum payment we can subtract to find her new balance:
            <md>\$915.66-\$25 = \$890.66</md>
            Her new balance will be $890.66.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </subsection>

  <exercises>
    <!-- Sources: Exercises 1 through 6 and 15 are adapted from Benjamin
    Jackson's MATH 105 lecture slides, clicker questions, lecture notes,
    and warm-up (Objective 4 and the Objectives 1-4 review), Walla Walla
    University, Fall 2026. Exercises 7 and 8 are based on his MATH 105
    written-homework question library (Fall 2026). Exercises 9 through 14
    and 16 through 20 are new.
    -->
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-finance-loans-noah-withdraws"><!--  #1 (Obj 4 warm-up: Noah cashes out, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Noah from <xref ref="exer-finance-annuity-mia-noah"/> now wants to <em>withdraw</em> $2,000 at the end of each of the next 3 years from an account paying 5% interest compounded annually. After the third withdrawal, the account will be empty.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Complete the table to find how much Noah needs in the account today. Each withdrawal is a lump sum, so find its present value as in <xref ref="sec-finance-compound-interest"/>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Noah takes out $6,000 in all. Why does he need less than $6,000 today?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Withdrawal</cell>
            <cell>Years from today</cell>
            <cell>Needed today</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 1</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 2</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 3</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Total</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Noah needs $5,446.50 today; see the table.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The money waiting in the account keeps earning interest until it is withdrawn, so the interest pays for part of the $6,000.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Withdrawal</cell>
            <cell>Years from today</cell>
            <cell>Needed today</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 1</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell><m>\$2{,}000/(1.05)^1 \approx \$1{,}904.76</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 2</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell><m>\$2{,}000/(1.05)^2 \approx \$1{,}814.06</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 3</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell><m>\$2{,}000/(1.05)^3 \approx \$1{,}727.68</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Total</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>$5,446.50</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #2 (Obj 4 lecture notes: checking the formula on Noah) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the present value of an annuity formula to find the amount Noah needs today in <xref ref="exer-finance-loans-noah-withdraws"/>. Does it match your table?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>d = \$2{,}000</m>, <m>r/n = 0.05</m>, and <m>nt = 3</m>.
          <md>
            P = \$2{,}000\left[\frac{1 - (1.05)^{-3}}{0.05}\right] = \$2{,}000(2.723248) \approx \$5{,}446.50
          </md>
          Yes, it matches. The formula adds up the present values of all three withdrawals in one calculation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #3 (Obj 4 clicker: which equation, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You have $80,000 in an annuity paying 4% interest compounded monthly, and you want to make equal monthly withdrawals for the next 15 years. Which equation gives the amount you can withdraw each month? Then solve it.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$80{,}000 = d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.04}{12}\right)^{-15}}{\frac{0.04}{12}}\right]</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$80{,}000 = d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.04}{12}\right)^{-180}}{\frac{0.04}{12}}\right]</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$80{,}000 = d\left[\frac{1-(1.04)^{-15}}{0.04}\right]</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>P = \$80{,}000\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.04}{12}\right)^{-180}}{\frac{0.04}{12}}\right]</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The answer is (b). There are <m>nt = 12(15) = 180</m> withdrawals, the monthly rate is <m>0.04/12</m>, and the $80,000 is the present value, so the unknown is <m>d</m>. Choice (a) uses 15 instead of 180 withdrawals, (c) uses a yearly rate and yearly withdrawals, and (d) puts $80,000 in place of the payment <m>d</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$80{,}000 =\amp d(135.192149)</mrow>
            <mrow>d =\amp \frac{\$80{,}000}{135.192149} \approx \$591.75</mrow>
          </md>
          You can withdraw $591.75 each month.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #4 (Obj 4 slides: Garcias' lease, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A family leases a car for 3 years at $380 per month. If money is worth 6% per year compounded monthly, what is the equivalent cash payment of this lease today? How much of the lease payments is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>d = \$380</m>, <m>r/n = 0.06/12 = 0.005</m>, and <m>nt = 36</m>.
          <md>
            P = \$380\left[\frac{1 - (1.005)^{-36}}{0.005}\right] = \$380(32.871016) \approx \$12{,}490.99
          </md>
          The lease is worth $12,490.99 in cash today. The family pays <m>\$380(36) = \$13{,}680</m> in all, so <m>\$13{,}680 - \$12{,}490.99 = \$1{,}189.01</m> is interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #5 (Obj 4 slides: Teresa, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Devon needs to borrow $18,000 for a car. One lender offers a 4-year loan at 5.4% APR, and another offers a 6-year loan at 6.6% APR, both with monthly payments. Find the monthly payment and the total interest for each loan. Which loan costs less?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>4-year loan:</em> <m>r/n = 0.054/12</m> and <m>nt = 48</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$18{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.054}{12}\right)^{-48}}{\frac{0.054}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$18{,}000 =\amp d(43.083153)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$417.80</mrow>
          </md>
          The interest is <m>48(\$417.80) - \$18{,}000 = \$2{,}054.40</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>6-year loan:</em> <m>r/n = 0.066/12</m> and <m>nt = 72</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$18{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.066}{12}\right)^{-72}}{\frac{0.066}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$18{,}000 =\amp d(59.320487)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$303.44</mrow>
          </md>
          The interest is <m>72(\$303.44) - \$18{,}000 = \$3{,}847.68</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 6-year loan has a lower monthly payment, but the 4-year loan costs $1,793.28 less in interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #6 (Obj 4 clickers: Sarah and Mike, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Jada and Luis buy a house for $320,000. They make a 15% down payment and finance the rest with a 30-year loan at 6.5% APR, paid monthly.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                What is their down payment?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is their monthly payment?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                One classmate got a monthly payment of $2,022.62, and another got $20,829.06. What did each one do wrong?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                How much interest will they pay over the life of the loan?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Instead, they pay $300 more than the required payment each month, which pays off the loan in about 242 payments. Treating this as exactly 242 payments, how much interest do they pay? How much do they save, and how many years early is the loan paid off?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>0.15(\$320{,}000) = \$48{,}000</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The loan is <m>\$320{,}000 - \$48{,}000 = \$272{,}000</m>, with <m>r/n = 0.065/12</m> and <m>nt = 360</m>.
                <md>
                  <mrow>\$272{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.065}{12}\right)^{-360}}{\frac{0.065}{12}}\right]</mrow>
                  <mrow>\$272{,}000 =\amp d(158.210820)</mrow>
                  <mrow>d \approx\amp \$1{,}719.23</mrow>
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                $2,022.62 is the payment on the full $320,000, without subtracting the down payment. $20,829.06 is a yearly payment: it uses <m>n = 1</m> and 30 payments instead of monthly payments.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>360(\$1{,}719.23) - \$272{,}000 = \$618{,}922.80 - \$272{,}000 = \$346{,}922.80</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                They pay <m>\$1{,}719.23 + \$300 = \$2{,}019.23</m> per month, so the interest is <m>242(\$2{,}019.23) - \$272{,}000 = \$216{,}653.66</m>. That saves <m>\$346{,}922.80 - \$216{,}653.66 = \$130{,}269.14</m>, and the loan ends <m>360 - 242 = 118</m> months, or about 9.8 years, early.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #7 (based on written 04a) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keisha buys a phone with no down payment and pays $45 per month for 24 months. The interest charged is 0.75% per month.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                What was the price of the phone?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                How much interest did Keisha pay?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                The monthly rate is already given, <m>r/n = 0.0075</m>, and <m>nt = 24</m>.
                <md>
                  P = \$45\left[\frac{1-(1.0075)^{-24}}{0.0075}\right] = \$45(21.889146) \approx \$985.01
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Keisha paid <m>\$45(24) = \$1{,}080</m>, so the interest was <m>\$1{,}080 - \$985.01 = \$94.99</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #8 (based on written 04b) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A couple took out a 30-year, $240,000 home loan at 6.75% compounded monthly. After making monthly payments for 4 years, they refinance the remaining balance with a new 20-year loan at 5.5% compounded monthly, with no closing costs.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                What was the monthly payment on the original loan?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the remaining balance on the original loan after 4 years of payments?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                How much interest have they paid in those 4 years?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Using your answer to (b) as the amount of the new loan, what is the new monthly payment? How much does the payment change?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                How much interest does the refinance save, compared with keeping the original loan for its full term?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <md>
                  <mrow>\$240{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.0675}{12}\right)^{-360}}{\frac{0.0675}{12}}\right]</mrow>
                  <mrow>\$240{,}000 =\amp d(154.178682)</mrow>
                  <mrow>d \approx\amp \$1{,}556.64</mrow>
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                There are <m>360 - 48 = 312</m> payments left.
                <md>
                  P = \$1{,}556.64\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.0675}{12}\right)^{-312}}{\frac{0.0675}{12}}\right] = \$1{,}556.64(146.887181) \approx \$228{,}650.46
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                They have paid off <m>\$240{,}000 - \$228{,}650.46 = \$11{,}349.54</m> of principal. They paid <m>48(\$1{,}556.64) = \$74{,}718.72</m> in all, so <m>\$74{,}718.72 - \$11{,}349.54 = \$63{,}369.18</m> was interest.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <md>
                  <mrow>\$228{,}650.46 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.055}{12}\right)^{-240}}{\frac{0.055}{12}}\right]</mrow>
                  <mrow>\$228{,}650.46 =\amp d(145.372649)</mrow>
                  <mrow>d \approx\amp \$1{,}572.86</mrow>
                </md>
                The payment goes up by only <m>\$1{,}572.86 - \$1{,}556.64 = \$16.22</m> per month.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The original loan would have cost <m>360(\$1{,}556.64) - \$240{,}000 = \$320{,}390.40</m> in interest. With the refinance, the interest is the $63,369.18 already paid plus the interest on the new loan:
                <md>
                  \$63{,}369.18 + \left[240(\$1{,}572.86) - \$228{,}650.46\right] = \$212{,}205.12.
                </md>
                The refinance saves <m>\$320{,}390.40 - \$212{,}205.12 = \$108{,}185.28</m> in interest.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #9 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A retiree has $300,000 in an account earning 5% compounded monthly. She wants to make equal monthly withdrawals for 25 years, ending with an empty account. How much can she withdraw each month? How much of what she withdraws is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.05/12</m> and <m>nt = 300</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$300{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.05}{12}\right)^{-300}}{\frac{0.05}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$300{,}000 =\amp d(171.060047)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$1{,}753.77</mrow>
          </md>
          She can withdraw $1,753.77 per month. Her withdrawals total <m>300(\$1{,}753.77) = \$526{,}131.00</m>, so $226,131.00 of it is interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #10 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A lottery winner can take $50,000 at the end of each year for 20 years, or a lump sum of $600,000 today.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                If money is worth 5% compounded annually, which option is worth more today?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Answer the same question if money is worth 7% compounded annually.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Compare the present value of the 20 payments with $600,000.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$50{,}000\left[\frac{1-(1.05)^{-20}}{0.05}\right] = \$50{,}000(12.462210) \approx \$623{,}110.52</m>, so the yearly payments are worth more.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$50{,}000\left[\frac{1-(1.07)^{-20}}{0.07}\right] = \$50{,}000(10.594014) \approx \$529{,}700.71</m>, so the lump sum is worth more. At a higher rate, money received later is worth less today.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #11 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sam finances an $18,000 boat with a 7-year loan at 7.2% APR, paid monthly. What is the monthly payment? What is the remaining balance after 3 years? How much of the principal has Sam paid off by then, and how does that compare with the total of the payments made?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.072/12 = 0.006</m> and <m>nt = 84</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$18{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-(1.006)^{-84}}{0.006}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$18{,}000 =\amp d(65.830026)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$273.43</mrow>
          </md>
          After 3 years, 48 payments remain:
          <md>
            P = \$273.43\left[\frac{1-(1.006)^{-48}}{0.006}\right] = \$273.43(41.598819) \approx \$11{,}374.37.
          </md>
          Sam has paid off <m>\$18{,}000 - \$11{,}374.37 = \$6{,}625.63</m> of principal, even though the payments total <m>36(\$273.43) = \$9{,}843.48</m>. The other $3,217.85 went to interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #12 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Ravi uses a credit card with 22.99% APR compounded daily to buy a $1,250 laptop. He makes no payments during the grace period, and his first payment is due 30 days after the grace period ends. The minimum payment is 2% of the balance (after interest) or $35, whichever is larger. What is his balance when the payment is due? What is his minimum payment? If he pays only the minimum, what is his new balance?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            \$1{,}250\left(1+\frac{0.2299}{365}\right)^{30} \approx \$1{,}273.84
          </md>
          Since <m>0.02(\$1{,}273.84) \approx \$25.48</m> is less than $35, his minimum payment is $35. His new balance is <m>\$1{,}273.84 - \$35 = \$1{,}238.84</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #13 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A credit card balance of $4,800 is charged 19.99% APR compounded daily for 31 days. The minimum payment is 3% of the balance (after interest) or $25, whichever is larger. Find the balance after interest, the minimum payment, and the new balance after the minimum payment.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            \$4{,}800\left(1+\frac{0.1999}{365}\right)^{31} \approx \$4{,}882.17
          </md>
          Since <m>0.03(\$4{,}882.17) \approx \$146.47</m> is more than $25, the minimum payment is $146.47. The new balance is <m>\$4{,}882.17 - \$146.47 = \$4{,}735.70</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #14 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A $200,000 home loan at 6% APR for 30 years has monthly payments of $1,199.10. Each month, the interest charged is one month's interest, <m>\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005</m> times the balance owed, and the rest of the payment goes to principal.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                How much of the first payment goes to interest, and how much to principal?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the balance after the first payment? How much of the second payment goes to interest?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use your answers to explain the pattern in <xref ref="fig-installment-loan-interest-principal"/>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Interest: <m>0.005(\$200{,}000) = \$1{,}000.00</m>. Principal: <m>\$1{,}199.10 - \$1{,}000.00 = \$199.10</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The balance is <m>\$200{,}000 - \$199.10 = \$199{,}800.90</m>, so the second month's interest is <m>0.005(\$199{,}800.90) \approx \$999.00</m>, and $200.10 goes to principal.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                At first, the balance is large, so most of each payment is interest. Each payment lowers the balance a little, so the next month's interest is a little smaller and a little more of the same payment goes to principal. The effect snowballs, so by the end of the loan most of each payment goes to principal.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #15 (Obj 1-4 review slides: what type of problem, new scenarios) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each situation, decide which section's formula applies: simple interest (<xref ref="sec-finance-simple-interest"/>), compound interest on a lump sum (<xref ref="sec-finance-compound-interest"/>), the future value of an annuity (<xref ref="sec-finance-savings-plans"/>), or the present value of an annuity (this section). You do not need to solve the problems.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                A couple saves $150 each month for 6 years toward a down payment on a house.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                You borrow $800 from a friend for 9 months at a simple interest rate of 12% per year.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A $10,000 inheritance is placed in a CD earning 4.5% compounded monthly for 7 years.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A $9,500 used car is paid off in 48 equal monthly payments.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First ask whether there is one lump sum or the same payment every period. Then ask whether the payments save up for later or pay off (or spend) money you have now.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Future value of an annuity: equal deposits that save up for later.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Simple interest: a lump sum whose interest is not compounded.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compound interest: a single lump sum that is compounded.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Present value of an annuity: equal payments that pay off an amount borrowed now (an installment loan).
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #16 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A couple can spend $2,400 a month on housing, but $450 of that goes to property taxes and insurance, and the rest is the loan payment. They have $60,000 for a down payment. If they can get a 30-year loan at 6.25% APR with monthly payments, what is the largest loan they can afford? What is the most they can pay for a house?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The loan payment is <m>d = \$2{,}400 - \$450 = \$1{,}950</m>, with <m>r/n = 0.0625/12</m> and <m>nt = 360</m>.
          <md>
            P = \$1{,}950\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.0625}{12}\right)^{-360}}{\frac{0.0625}{12}}\right] = \$1{,}950(162.412224) \approx \$316{,}703.84
          </md>
          They can borrow up to $316,703.84. With the down payment, they can pay up to <m>\$316{,}703.84 + \$60{,}000 = \$376{,}703.84</m> for a house.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #17 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Maya graduates owing $27,000 in student loans at 5.5% APR with monthly payments. Find her monthly payment and her total interest under a 10-year repayment plan and under a 20-year plan.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <em>10-year plan:</em> <m>nt = 120</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$27{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.055}{12}\right)^{-120}}{\frac{0.055}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$27{,}000 =\amp d(92.143582)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$293.02</mrow>
          </md>
          The interest is <m>120(\$293.02) - \$27{,}000 = \$8{,}162.40</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>20-year plan:</em> <m>nt = 240</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$27{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-\left(1+\frac{0.055}{12}\right)^{-240}}{\frac{0.055}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$27{,}000 =\amp d(145.372649)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$185.73</mrow>
          </md>
          The interest is <m>240(\$185.73) - \$27{,}000 = \$17{,}575.20</m>. The 20-year plan lowers the payment by $107.29 a month but more than doubles the interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #18 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Elena has a $3,600 credit card balance at 24% APR compounded monthly. She stops using the card and wants to pay it off with equal monthly payments. Find the monthly payment and the total interest if she pays it off in 1 year, and if she pays it off in 3 years.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.24/12 = 0.02</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>1 year:</em> <m>nt = 12</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$3{,}600 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-(1.02)^{-12}}{0.02}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$3{,}600 =\amp d(10.575341)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$340.41</mrow>
          </md>
          The interest is <m>12(\$340.41) - \$3{,}600 = \$484.92</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>3 years:</em> <m>nt = 36</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$3{,}600 =\amp d\left[\frac{1-(1.02)^{-36}}{0.02}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$3{,}600 =\amp d(25.488842)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$141.24</mrow>
          </md>
          The interest is <m>36(\$141.24) - \$3{,}600 = \$1{,}484.64</m>, about three times as much.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #19 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A family sets up a scholarship that will pay $4,000 at the end of each semester for 4 years. How much must they deposit now into an account paying 3% compounded semiannually? How much of the scholarship money comes from interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.03/2 = 0.015</m> and <m>nt = 8</m>.
          <md>
            P = \$4{,}000\left[\frac{1-(1.015)^{-8}}{0.015}\right] = \$4{,}000(7.485925) \approx \$29{,}943.70
          </md>
          They must deposit $29,943.70. The scholarship pays <m>8(\$4{,}000) = \$32{,}000</m>, so $2,056.30 comes from interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #20 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Jordan can afford a car payment of $450 per month on a 72-month loan at 7.2% APR and has a $3,000 trade-in to put toward the car. What is the largest loan Jordan can afford, and what is the most Jordan can spend on a car? How much interest would Jordan pay on that loan?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.072/12 = 0.006</m> and <m>nt = 72</m>.
          <md>
            P = \$450\left[\frac{1-(1.006)^{-72}}{0.006}\right] = \$450(58.325343) \approx \$26{,}246.40
          </md>
          Jordan can borrow up to $26,246.40, so with the trade-in Jordan can spend up to $29,246.40 on a car. The payments total <m>72(\$450) = \$32{,}400</m>, so the interest is <m>\$32{,}400 - \$26{,}246.40 = \$6{,}153.60</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
